// CARD a5-01 · SEE: Kjeld's portrait framed on a dark wall over his plate; on "stepped" the photo drains grey and slides out of the frame; OCTOBER 2004 sets beside it · SEAM in right
# cine act=after fx="code:@photo_drain;pic:lego_kjeld_1996_award;plate:KJELD;go:stepped;date:OCTOBER 2004;in:right"
{tense} In October two thousand and four, Kjeld stepped back after twenty-five years as chief **executive**.

// CARD a5-02 · SEE: Jørgen Vig Knudstorp speaking, the camera travelling in toward his face; on "man" his name card slides in at the top · SEAM out right
# cine act=after fx="code:@name_strap;pic:lego_knudstorp_2010;go:man;name:JØRGEN VIG KNUDSTORP;role:CEO from October 2004 · the author of the warning;fx:.5;fy:.1;out:right"
The new chief executive was the man who had written the **warning**.

// CARD a5-03 · SEE: one time line across the frame; real portraits pinned on at their years: Ole Kirk 1932, Godtfred 1957, Kjeld 1979, Knudstorp 2004; on "outside" the gap note sets: the first outsider in a generation · SEAM in right
# cine act=after fx="code:@timeline_pins;items:lego_ole_kirk_portrait^0,0,1,1^.14^1932^OLE KIRK · FOUNDER^Knudstorp~lego_godtfred_cropped^0,0,1,1^.38^1957^GODTFRED · SON^first~lego_kjeld_1996_award^0,0,1,1^.62^1979^KJELD · GRANDSON^family~lego_knudstorp_2010^0,0,1,1^.84^2004^KNUDSTORP · OUTSIDER^generation;in:right"
Knudstorp was thirty-five, and the first chief executive from outside the family in a **generation**.

// CARD a5-04 · SEE: the real Bloomberg story's header at the top; below it his verbatim words light from grey to ink as the quote voice reads them, over hands counting notes · SEAM out right · HOLD
# cine act=after fx="code:@post_quote;doc:lego_doc_headline_businessstandard_2013;crop:0,0,.62,.065;bg:stock_money_counting_machine_03;q:“At first I actually said, let's not talk about strategy, let's talk about an action plan, to address the debt, to get the cash flow.”;go:cash;span:5.5;size:60;by:KNUDSTORP · 2011 interview, via Bloomberg · voice actor;out:right"
He said his first job wasn't strategy. It was **cash**.
@knudstorp: At first I actually said, let's not talk about strategy, let's talk about an action plan, to address the debt, to get the cash flow.

// CARD a5-05 · SEE: thick bundles of notes drop onto a dark desk and stack while the count runs to DKK 800M; tags set beside it: A LOAN · FROM KIRKBI, THE FAMILY'S HOLDING COMPANY · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@cash_pile;img:stock_money_wallet_10__060;n:14;go:lent;val:800;pre:DKK;unit:M;lab:LENT TO LEGO · 2004;tags:FROM KIRKBI~THE FAMILY'S HOLDING COMPANY;in:right"
The family's holding company, KIRKBI, lent LEGO eight hundred million **kroner**.

// CARD a5-06 · SEE: hands counting notes with a calculator, dim; a red tag hangs on a string reading GIFT; on "loan" it spins round and its back reads LOAN · CIBOR + 3% · DUE DECEMBER 2011 · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@gift_tag_flip;clip:stock_money_broll_10;front:GIFT;back:LOAN;terms:CIBOR + 3%~DUE DECEMBER 2011~LOCKED WHILE EQUITY < 40%;go:loan;out:right"
{wry} Not a gift. A loan, with interest, due in twenty **eleven**. (J)

// CARD a5-07 · SEE: an empty factory line (AI moment art) on the desk as a print; on "fifth" it tears along a ragged line and a fifth of it slides away: −20% · DKK 1,523M · ABOUT 1,000 JOBS · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@split_note;img:lego_art_factory_line_empty;cut:.8;lab1:WHAT STAYED;lab2:−20% · DKK 1,523M;at:fifth;in:right"
Costs were cut by a fifth, more than one and a half billion kroner, and about a thousand jobs **went**.

// CARD a5-08 · SEE: a heap of bricks fills the frame under a counter reading 12,000+; on "cut" a sweep clears most of them out of frame and the counter runs down to 7,000 · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@pile_sweep;bg:lego_brick_pile_mixed;go:cut;from:12000;to:7000;n:150;keep:.45;lab:DIFFERENT PARTS;out:right"
The parts library was cut back to about seven **thousand**.

// CARD a5-09 · SEE: a sorting machine working behind; a wall of colour chips, one per shade; on "half" every other chip drops out and falls · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@colour_halve;clip:lego_clip_factory_sorting;n:100;go:half;lab:ABOUT 100 COLOURS → ABOUT 50;in:right"
So the colours were cut roughly in **half**.

// CARD a5-10 · MOTIF · SEE: a clear box of Technic parts sorted by kind, compartment by compartment; the brick drops in and lands in its own slot; EARN ITS PLACE sets above · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@brick_drop;bg:lego_technic_bits;go:earn;x:.5;y:.55;h:.1;lab:EVERY PART EARNS ITS PLACE;lx:.5;z:.25;move:right;out:right"
And from then on, a new part had to earn its **place**.

// CARD a5-11 · SEE: the real log-flume ride at LEGOLAND Billund, the camera travelling along it · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@reel;under:lego_legoland_logflume;ua:0;move:right;z:.4;in:right"
Then the parks went **too**.

// CARD a5-12 · SEE: the real castle courtyard at LEGOLAND Billund; on "sold" a red placard is planted in front of it: SOLD · CONTROL · €375M · JULY 2005 · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@sign_on_footage;clip:lego_legoland_castle;sign:SOLD;sub:€375M · JULY 2005;go:sold;x:.66;y:.42;out:right"
In July two thousand and five, LEGO sold control of LEGOLAND for three hundred and seventy-five million **euros**.

// CARD a5-13 · SEE: the real Miniland harbour at LEGOLAND; a ring of shares is drawn round it; on "kept" the 70% sold to Blackstone and Merlin swings away, LEGO 15% and KIRKBI 15% stay lit · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@pie_on_photo;pic:lego_legoland_modern;slices:BLACKSTONE/MERLIN^70^0^#6d655b~LEGO^15^1^#f2c14e~KIRKBI^15^1^#e08a3a;go:kept;in:right"
LEGO and its family holding company kept thirty **percent**.

// CARD a5-14 · SEE: a boy building a tall tower of bricks on a living-room floor, dim; one bar hangs below a zero line in red, −1,931 (2004); on "profit" it swings up through zero to +505 in gold (2005) · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@swing_bar;bg:lego_clip_blocks_boy;a:-1931;b:505;go:profit;alab:2004 · NET;blab:2005 · NET;unit:M DKK;out:right"
{warm} In two thousand and five, LEGO made a profit again: five hundred and five million kroner, **net**.

// CARD a5-15 · SEE: a deserted outlet shopfront with empty windows and a faded sign, the camera sliding past · SEAM in right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_closed_outlet_shopfront;move:right;z:.25;in:right"
Sales grew only a little. The swing came from what LEGO stopped **doing**.
